														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1831120">
									

<p>While this is getting off-topic pretty quickly&#8230; well, the fact is, Gattsune, every so often we have some user (usually new) who misunderstands why we say stuff is 'cliche' or 'too similar to XYZ other article'. This user tends to annoy pretty much everyone else. Especially when, for consistency purposes, they start downvoting many (often very good) SCP articles for the crime of being vaguely similar to some other article. Which is what usually happens. (It used to be worse when the Cliche List was actively maintained, because then such users would <em>reference that</em>.)</p>
<p>If the similarities bug you, fine. But it sounds like you're downvoting because you've misunderstood 'modern SCP theory'. Which is understandable, but it's also going to be really aggravating for everyone if you stick to your guns on that.</p>
<p>In other words:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, I cannot in good conscience upvote a skip that is otherwise a near carbon copy of SCP-1322. It needs a lot more to stand on its own feet. -1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is bad logic. This has similar story elements to Hartle, but <em>those elements were never, ever original to Hartle</em>. They're boilerplate, standard stuff for <em>this kind of story</em>, and in both SCPs, they're <em>not the point</em>.</p>
<p>It's like hating on tragedies because they require the main character to be admirable (a king, CEO, etc), go through a fall from grace due to hubris, and die in the end. SUCH CLICHES AMIRITE?</p>
<p>If you can't differentiate between story structure and cliche, you're going to have a very difficult time writing yourself. Because literally almost <em>everything</em> has been done already. Everything on the SCP wiki has also been done in some way somewhere else. Therefore, it's the execution that matters.</p>

							</div>

								
					
